The only difference is in the format: Go's isoLayout "Z07:00" outputs "Z" for UTC, whereas Python outputs "+00:00". The times are identical. In TestPythonReference, do string comparison with a normalization that treats Z == +00:00. Note: in the golden test, the golden values were generated in Python → always "+00:00", and Go formats UTC as "Z" → mismatch! But the golden test seems to have passed (no golden failures shown at the end... it's only the tail end of the output). Wait — the golden's n0 value is in a format like "2020-01-15T00:01:00+00:00". Go's Format(isoLayout) for a UTC time → "2020-01-15T00:01:00Z". Mismatch → TestGolden should fail. But the end of the output only shows the TestPythonReference failures. Let me look at the full output. Perhaps TestGolden failed but got cut off at the tail. Run it with the full output.

Fix approach: add a helper fmtRef(t time.Time) string that formats it and replaces the trailing "Z" with "+00:00" when the offset is zero. Python's isoformat for a +00:00 timezone outputs "+00:00". For a +01:00 offset, Go outputs "+01:00", same as Python. So:

```go
func refFormat(t time.Time) string {
	s := t.Format(isoLayout)
	if off, _ := t.Zone(); off == 0 {
		s = strings.TrimSuffix(s, "Z") + "+00:00"
	}
	return s
}
```

But note: does isoLayout with ".999999999" output fractional seconds in groups? Python always outputs 6-digit microseconds when non-zero. For example, a base with .999000... but Next zeros out seconds/micros, so fractional seconds never appear in the result. Therefore the results always have no fractional part. Go's ".999999999" outputs nothing when zero. Good.

Use refFormat in both the golden comparison and the Python reference comparison.

